OBJECTIVE: all three forms of `clear arp-cache` remove the dynamic entries they name and no
others; the removal is real in the DATAPLANE (the DUT re-ARPs and stops forwarding), not merely
in the table read-back; a statically configured entry is NOT removed by any of them; and the
cache repopulates normally afterwards.

SCOPE NOTE, stated deliberately: `clear arp-cache` is an EXEC command with no configuration
footprint.  The "prove removal from running-config and persistence across a reboot" floor
(memory `satisfactory-level-of-testing`) has no object here -- there is nothing to remove and
nothing to persist.  The two equivalent properties ARE proven instead:
  - the effect is not sticky: the cache repopulates on the next resolution (steps 4, 6, 7);
  - the volatile cache does not survive a reboot anyway.  Measured in this group's case 1 on the
    same DUT: after the `reload` in T33248 step 6, `show arp` held ONLY the static entry
    (T33248/work/run1.log step 6).  Quoted here rather than costing this case a second reboot.


