5. A PLATFORM BEHAVIOUR THAT CHANGED THE METHOD  (found at step 0, and it matters)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The obvious way to populate a DUT's ARP cache is to send it an ARP request.  RFC 826 says a
device that is the TARGET of an ARP request adds the sender to its table.  THIS DUT DOES NOT.

  leg L0a-pop-eth3: 2 x `who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 192.168.10.2` from eth3
                    -> DUT answered both: `ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43`
  leg L0b-pop-eth4: 2 x `who-has 192.168.48.1 tell 192.168.48.2` from eth4
                    -> DUT answered both: `ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43`
  awplus# show arp
  IP Address      LL Address       Interface            Port        Type
  192.168.48.9    0011.2233.4499   vlan48               port1.0.48  static
                    <- NEITHER sender was learned.  Nothing dynamic at all.

=> On sin_x96056-20261012-24 a dynamic ARP entry is created only for a neighbour that ANSWERS an
   ARP request the DUT ITSELF sent.  Being asked is not enough.

Consequence for the method, and for every later ARP case: a leg that needs a dynamic entry must
ANSWER, not merely ask.  `tools/arpleg.py` was extended for this with `--answer IP=MAC` (one
background responder per `--answer-iface`, sharing one IP=MAC table, each seeing only its own
wire) and `--announce` (gratuitous ARP).  The reason is written into the Answerer docstring.
This also gives the group the ability to STOP answering on demand, which is how T33246's
web-auth poll timeout will be driven.

With the responder, learning works first time:

  tool: sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L0c-learn --out /tmp/ck1747/T33247 \
          --iface eth3 --watch eth4 --probe 192.168.48.2 --sender-ip 192.168.10.2 \
          --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 5 \
          --answer 192.168.48.2=00:11:22:33:44:02,192.168.10.2=00:11:22:33:44:01 \
          --answer-iface eth3,eth4 --wait 6
  answering ARP for: 192.168.10.2=00:11:22:33:44:01, 192.168.48.2=00:11:22:33:44:02 on eth3,eth4
  answerer sent 1 ARP reply/replies (eth3:0, eth4:1)
  cap L0c-learn-eth4.pcap: 7 frame(s)
    x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.2 :9 [ours]  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:02]
    x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.2 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:02]
    x1  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.2 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:02    [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]

  SECONDARY OBSERVATION: that ARP request was UNICAST to 00:11:22:33:44:02, not broadcast, even
  though `show arp` had held no entry for 192.168.48.2.  The DUT had seen that MAC in the earlier
  L0b leg.  So the DUT retains some knowledge of a sender it will not show in `show arp` -- one
  more reason not to grade an ARP case on that table.  When the address is genuinely unknown the
  request IS broadcast (every baseline leg below shows `-> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff`).

  A second learn leg (L0d-learn10, same shape from eth4 towards 192.168.10.2, answering on eth3)
  produced the vlan10 entry the same way, with a broadcast request and one reply.


