VERDICT: PASS -- all three implemented forms of `clear arp-cache` (single address, unqualified,
`global`) were proven to clear exactly the dynamic entries they name and no others, in the
DATAPLANE and not only in `show arp`: after each clear the DUT broadcast ARP requests for the
cleared address and forwarded 0 of 5 frames, while an address whose entry survived still
forwarded 5 of 5 with no ARP at all.  The single-address and unqualified forms are distinguished
on the wire by the DUT's own ICMP unreachables, which only the surviving entry made possible.  A
statically configured entry is explicitly protected ("% Skipping ... as it is statically
assigned.") and still forwarded after every clear.  The cache repopulates normally afterwards.
The `vrf` form is NOT EXERCISED (no VRF exists on the DUT; that is group D's T43859) and is
recorded as such rather than claimed.  Two platform behaviours worth carrying: the command is
`clear arp-cache`, not `clear arp`; and this DUT does NOT learn an ARP entry from a request it
merely answers, which changes how any ARP case must populate a cache.
